arlington.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-08","committee":"Adults Scrutiny Committee","time":"1.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.darlington.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-16","committee":"Health and Housing Scrutiny Committee","time":"11.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.darlington.gov.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Overall in-year position \u00a32.7m worse than Medium Term Financial Plan","lead":"An adverse variance of this scale in-year suggests either the MTFP planning was optimistic, new pressures have emerged since the plan was set (February 2026), or budget control is weaker than planned. It raises questions about forecast accuracy and demand\u2026","iso":"2026-09-08","paper_title":"260908 RG RBM Qtr 1 Appendices","paper_url":"https://darlington.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s29148/29148.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"People Group running \u00a32.7m projected deficit, the largest departmental overspend","lead":"People services are mandated, demand-led, and growing nationally; a deficit of this scale suggests structural budget stress rather than one-off pressures, and indicates the council is struggling to contain costs in its most volatile service area.","iso":"2026-09-08","paper_title":"260908 RG RBM Qtr 1 Appendices","paper_url":"https://darlington.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s29148/29148.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Council plans to withdraw \u00a33.7m from general fund reserves to balance 2026/27 budget","lead":"Reserve depletion signals declining financial flexibility and reduces the council's capacity to borrow or absorb future shocks.","iso":"2026-09-08","paper_title":"260908 RG RBM Qtr 1 Appendices","paper_url":"https://darlington.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s29148/29148.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-08","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"260908 RG RBM Qtr 1 Appendices","paper_url":"https://darlington.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s29148/29148.pdf","headline":"Overall in-year position \u00a32.7m worse than Medium Term Financial Plan","detail":"The comparison between the Q1 projection and the MTFP shows an adverse variance of \u00a32,726k. This indicates the council is tracking significantly below its own financial plan for the year.","category":"overspend","matters":"An adverse variance of this scale in-year suggests either the MTFP planning was optimistic, new pressures have emerged since the plan was set (February 2026), or budget control is weaker than planned. It raises questions about forecast accuracy and demand management.","figures":"\u00a32,726k decline compared with 2026-30 MTFP","quote":"Improvement / (Decline) compared with MTFP (2,726)","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-08","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"260908 RG RBM Qtr 1 Appendices","paper_url":"https://darlington.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s29148/29148.pdf","headline":"People Group running \u00a32.7m projected deficit, the largest departmental overspend","detail":"The People Group (likely social services including adult and children's services) is projected to end the year with a \u00a32,654k deficit. This is by far the largest departmental budget pressure, more than seven times larger than any other service area.","category":"overspend","matters":"People services are mandated, demand-led, and growing nationally; a deficit of this scale suggests structural budget stress rather than one-off pressures, and indicates the council is struggling to contain costs in its most volatile service area.","figures":"People Group deficit \u00a32,654k; total departmental deficits \u00a33,079k","quote":"People Group (2,654)","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-08","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"260908 RG RBM Qtr 1 Appendices","paper_url":"https://darlington.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s29148/29148.pdf","headline":"Council plans to withdraw \u00a33.7m from general fund reserves to balance 2026/27 budget","detail":"The Medium Term Financial Plan includes an approved net contribution from balances of \u00a33,704k in 2026/27. This is a direct drawdown of reserves to balance the revenue budget, reducing the council's financial flexibility and liquidity position.","category":"reserves","matters":"Reserve depletion signals declining financial flexibility and reduces the council's capacity to borrow or absorb future shocks.","figures":"\u00a33,704k approved net contribution from balances; opening balance \u00a39,787k; closing balance before departmental issues \u00a36,083k","quote":"Approved net contribution from balances (3,704)","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-08","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"260908 RG RBM Qtr 1 Appendices APP2F","paper_url":"https://darlington.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s29029/29029.pdf","headline":"Primary schools projected to carry deficits totalling \u00a3411k into 2026/27.","detail":"Red Hall Primary is projected to close at a deficit of \u00a3207k (17% of its budget), and Rise Carr College, Clifton House & Eldon House at \u00a3344k (14% of budget). Together these deficits total \u00a3411k across primary schools shown, meaning the primary sector as a whole is projected into deficit.","category":"demand","matters":"School deficits are a direct liability to the council and indicate budget management failure in schools that cannot be sustained; the council may need to fund these deficits from reserves or other sources.","figures":"Red Hall Primary: closing balance (207) = (17% of budget); Rise Carr College, Clifton House & Eldon House: closing balance (344) = (14% of budget); Primary Total deficit: (411)","quote":"Red Hall Primary | (18) | 1,243 | 1,225 | (207) | (17%) | Rise Carr College, Clifton House & Eldon House | (224) | 2,384 | 2,160 | (344) | (14%) | Primary Total | 96 | 7,418 | 7,514 | (411) |","material":false},{"iso":"2026-09-08","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"260908 RG RBM Qtr 1 Appendices HRA3","paper_url":"https://darlington.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s29030/29030.pdf","headline":"HRA reserves drawn down \u00a34.5m in quarter, exceeding budget by \u00a3819k","detail":"In-year balance contribution of \u00a34.482m exceeds the budget of \u00a33.663m by \u00a3819k. Opening HRA balances of \u00a318.730m are projected to close the quarter at \u00a314.248m, a 24% reduction. If this depletion rate continues, HRA reserves would be exhausted within a year.","category":"reserves","matters":"Rapid reserve depletion indicates the HRA is not generating sufficient retained surplus to fund capital investment or cover contingencies, threatening long-term solvency and the ability to maintain the housing stock.","figures":"Opening balance \u00a318.730m, closing \u00a314.248m, budgeted draw \u00a33.663m, actual draw \u00a34.482m, variance \u00a3819k","quote":"In year contribution to/(from) balances (3,663) 0 (3,663) (4,482) (819)","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-08"},"name":"Darlington UA","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-17","since_iso":"2026-08-18","distress":false,"headline":"Overall in-year position \u00a32.7m worse than Medium Term Financial Plan","lead":"An adverse variance of this scale in-year suggests either the MTFP planning was optimistic, new pressures have emerged since the plan was set (February 2026), or budget control is weaker than planned. It raises questions about forecast accuracy and demand\u2026","also":["36 other findings in this window","3 papers caught"],"watch":"Cabinet on 2026-10-06","n_findings":37,"n_documents":3,"top_paper_title":"260908 RG RBM Qtr 1 Appendices","top_paper_url":"https://darlington.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s29148/29148.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":89,"news_drafts_total":3}}